<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Los Angeles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Los Angeles is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and the second largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was founded in 1781 as a Spanish farming pueblo on the Los Angeles River, remained a small ranching town under Mexican rule, and passed to the United States in 1848. Railways, oil, the film industry and aerospace later made it a vast metropolis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/los-angeles.jpg" alt="Los Angeles" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gnaphron · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hollywood_sign_(8485145044).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Most of the city lies in the Los Angeles Basin beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, extending partly through the Santa Monica Mountains and north into the San Fernando Valley. The eastern end of the Santa Monica Mountains runs from Downtown to the Pacific and separates the basin from the valley. The city covers about 1,302 square kilometres in all, including 88 square kilometres of water, and extends 71 km from north to south and 47 km from east to west. Its highest point is Mount Lukens, at 1,547 metres, in the foothills of the San Gabriel Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Los Angeles Basin and the San Fernando Valley were dominated by the Tongva, later also called the Gabrieleño. The historic centre of Tongva power was the settlement of Yaanga, a name translated as 'place of the poison oak', which became the site where the Spanish founded the pueblo. Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo claimed the area for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1542, and Gaspar de Portolà and the Franciscan missionary Juan Crespí reached the present site on 2 August 1769.</p><p>On 4 September 1781, 44 settlers known as Los Pobladores founded El Pueblo de Nuestra Señora la Reina de los Ángeles, 'The Town of Our Lady the Queen of the Angels'. The town became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> in 1821. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1848, Los Angeles and the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a>, and the city was incorporated on 4 April 1850, five months before California became a state. Oil discovered in the 1890s brought rapid growth, and the Los Angeles Aqueduct, completed in 1913, brought water from Eastern California.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Los Angeles is the most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and the commercial, financial and cultural centre of Southern California, with 3,898,747 residents counted in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is the county seat of Los Angeles County and a charter city governed by a mayor and a city council. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> over 200 languages are spoken there. The city hosted the Summer Olympics in 1932 and 1984, and is one of the oldest and largest hubs of film and television production.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Los Angeles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Migration created a large diaspora in the United States, and the gang Mara Salvatrucha (MS-13), formed by young Salvadorans in <b>Los Angeles</b>, spread to Central America through deportations in the 1990s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their languages, of which there are 7 distinct ones and over 100 dialects, are tone languages spoken in the Sierra Norte, the Central Valleys, the Southern Sierra, the Isthmus of Tehuantepec and along the Pacific coast, and through migration also in Mexico City and <b>Los Angeles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed, rioting broke out in the Watts district of <b>Los Angeles</b>, where 34 people died; in the summer of 1967 disorders struck many cities, and 43 people died in Detroit alone.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the Populist and Progressive movements took up the Swiss devices: South Dakota adopted the initiative and referendum in 1898, Oregon in 1902 and California in 1911, and <b>Los Angeles</b> introduced the recall in 1903.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Architects have copied or echoed it, from the unfinished Nazi Kongresshalle in Nuremberg and Mussolini's Palazzo della Civiltà Italiana to the <b>Los Angeles</b> Memorial Coliseum and the Vancouver Public Library.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The orchestra played it at the closing ceremony of the 1932 Summer Olympics in <b>Los Angeles</b> as the flame went out.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a></li></ul></section>
